## (5) OFFLINE POSITIVE CONTROL (Linux host, no crash — sequence runs to completion)

Native test step `c2_diag_tests` (build.zig:123) runs the SAME c2_diag_sequence against the real vendored
QuickJS; here JS_NewRuntime2 returns, so every malloc completes and each 'M' must close with 'm'/'!'. The
new c2_diag_test.zig block "a completed heap_malloc always closes its M marker" asserts that NO 'M' between
`new_rt ` and `rt=` dangles. Observed offline witness (on-disk /tmp/c2_diag_<pid>.log, rc=0 line):

    enter heap_ok alloc_ok free_ok new_rt MmMmMmMmMmMmMmMmrt=0x2235f28 opaque_ok limits_ok new_raw RFraw_ok new_diag RFFRFRFdiag_rc=0 ctx=0x223ef58

Reading: alive, `new_rt ` is followed by 8 matched `Mm` pairs (malloc lifecycles, capped at MF_TAG_CAP=8)
then `rt=`. This is what licenses the LIVE "dangling M" reading — it is NOT "the close tag never compiled."
(Contrast the §41 entry-only build in the same dir, which read `new_rt MMMMMMMMrt=`.)

