## (7) reading

    offline alive : new_rt MmMmMmMmMmMmMmMm rt=   (8 mallocs through mf, EACH returned, then returns)
    live dying    : new_rt MmMmMm                     (3 mallocs through mf, ALL THREE returned cleanly, then reads die at 6947ms, no rt=)

All three `heap_malloc` calls COMPLETED — each 'M' is closed by a lowercase 'm', i.e. HeapAlloc handed back a
block each time. **No dangling 'M'.** A heap/environment-side death (our allocator or GetProcessHeap failing
to return under the injected remote thread) is EXCLUDED: that would leave a dangling 'M', and there is none.
§41's fork resolves to the ENGINE side — the death is in QuickJS's own JS_NewRuntime2 code, after the 3rd
allocation returned and before a 4th heap_malloc was entered. The 1892 successful polls spanning 72.4ms →
6947.1ms never advanced the static past `MmMmMm`: ~6.9s of ZERO further allocation activity.

Honest boundaries:
  - rc=3 is the PROCESS exit code, NOT a step code (the WARNING + §38.5 retraction apply — not "the Date
    intrinsic"). buffer log and the on-disk C:/temp/c2_diag_8288.log are both '' (written only at finish:,
    never reached); the static is the only channel that carried text, exactly as §38 designed.
  - This is ONE shot; "3" is a position, not shown stable across shots (§40's budget rule bars a reflexive
    re-fire). It does not make JS_NewRuntime2 survive — C2 stays blocked.
  - The next probe crosses a capability line: the window that matters now (3rd alloc returned → 4th alloc, a
    6.9s no-allocation gap) is entirely INSIDE QuickJS's own code. Our allocator observes only allocation
    boundaries, so read-only instrumentation has hit its ceiling. Seeing into that gap requires hooking
    QuickJS code = a capability escalation (升级授权), which the current read-only level does not grant and
    which was NOT done here.

## (8) read-only self-attestation

    - no hook/Interceptor/patchCode/writeByteArray/Memory.protect on code lines; no new capability added.
    - the third phase ('m'/'!') is a one-char write into the DLL's OWN g_diag_log static — same operation
      class as §41's entry 'M', same authorization level.
    - heap_malloc's alloc/free LOGIC is unchanged; only the marker emission split entry vs outcome.
    - mf callbacks were OBSERVED via tags only; the disk file still produced nothing (write only at finish:,
      never reached).
